# Rocdown site configuration

Place `rocdown.toml` at the documentation content root when you need site metadata,
hashed assets, or curated navigation. The file is optional: a directory of
`.rocdown` pages, or a single `.rocdown` file whose parent is the content root,
is enough for `rocdown build`, `rocdown check`, and `rocdown view`.

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- build report.rocdown --output dist
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- run report.rocdown
```

## Example

```toml
[site]
title = "Rocci"
description = "Rocci — Composable authoring for applications and content"
base_url = "https://rocci.dev"
language = "en"
repository = "https://github.com/koliyo/rocci"
social_image = "/assets/og.png"
subtitle = "Composable authoring for applications and content"
footer = "Rocci is experimental software."

[build]
output = "../dist/rocci.dev"
assets = "assets"

[[nav]]
label = "Start here"
items = ["index", "five-minutes"]

[[nav]]
label = "Reference"
items = ["reference/rocci", "reference/rocdown"]
```

Unknown keys are errors.

## Snippets and examples

Optional snippet roots are resolved relative to the content root. They still
cannot escape the repository parent, and include paths cannot contain `..`,
NUL, or absolute locations.

```toml
[snippets]
roots = ["snippets", "../examples"]

[examples]
timeout_ms = 30000
allow_network = false
```

`:include` reads a file as nested Rocdown (`.rocdown`, no `@page`) or as a
code literal. Named regions use `docs-region: NAME` / `docs-region-end: NAME`
markers. Line ranges emit warning `RD2504`. Cycles are `RD2505`. Included files
are watch inputs for `rocdown view`.

`rocdown check` validates components and includes without Roc. Untested
`:example` records warn (`RD2601`). Example commands never run during
`rocdown build` or `rocdown check`.

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- test docs
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- test docs --update
```

`rocdown test` runs declared `test` argument lists with cwd at the content root, a
timeout, no extra environment, and no shell expansion. `--update` rewrites
golden files. Failures are diagnostics (`RD2603`); `dist/` is not written.
Fenced code is never executed. Default CSP allows the hashed Cmd/Ctrl-K
palette (`script-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'`) and still forbids
`unsafe-eval` and `unsafe-inline`. Tab panels are stacked sections, not a
JavaScript tablist.

## Site fields

| Field | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `title` | `Documentation` | Header, metadata, and generated index title |
| `description` | Empty | Default page and agent-index description |
| `base_url` | Empty | Canonical, sitemap, and absolute discovery URLs |
| `language` | `en` | Document language |
| `repository` | Empty | Source link in the header |
| `social_image` | Empty | Site-relative Open Graph image path |
| `favicon` | Empty | Site-relative favicon path, typically an SVG |
| `apple_touch_icon` | Empty | Site-relative Apple touch icon path |
| `subtitle` | Empty | Short header tagline beside the brand |
| `footer` | Empty | Article footer text |
| `csp` | Strict static default | Content-Security-Policy for generated HTML |

`base_url` must use HTTP or HTTPS. A trailing slash is normalized away.
Empty `subtitle` and `footer` omit those chrome pieces. Leave `csp` empty to use
the default policy: hashed `goto.js` from `'self'`, fetches of `pages.json` and
same-origin HTML from `'self'`, no `unsafe-eval` or `unsafe-inline`, styles and
images from `'self'`. `live` pages override that page's CSP so Datastar can load
and connect to the island service.

## HTTP fields

Optional `[http]` configures the island service used by `live` pages.

| Field | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `service_origin` | Empty | Absolute origin for Datastar `connect-src` and `@post` URLs. Empty means same-origin (the operator reverse-proxies `/actions/` to the service). |
| `service` | Empty | Sibling `.rocci` app to run instead of the generated island dispatcher. |

`service_origin` must be empty or start with `http://` or `https://`. A trailing
slash is normalized away.

## Page kinds

Each published page is one of:

| Kind | Meaning | CDN | Datastar |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `static` | Markdown / `@page` / `:name[params]` only | Widget forest HTML and hashed assets | No |
| `hydrate` | Pure Rocci (`@component`, `@render`, `@css`, `@roc`) plus Markdown `@{expr}` | Initial island HTML spliced at build | No |
| `live` | Rocci plus `@get:view` / `@method:fragment` / `@method:command` / `@context` / `@init` or `import Datastar` | Initial HTML plus Datastar.js | Yes |

`rocdown check` accepts all three. Default `rocdown build` accepts `live` and
emits the island service manifest. `rocdown build --cdn-only` errors with
`RD2302` when any published page is `live`, so a CDN publish cannot ship action
buttons with no service.

## Two-artifact publish

A hybrid site emits:

1. **CDN tree** — page HTML, hashed `/assets/` files, discovery files. `static`
   and `hydrate` pages include hashed `goto.js` (Cmd/Ctrl-K) with
   `script-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'`. They do not include Datastar.
   `live` pages include hashed Datastar.js and a loosened CSP (`connect-src`
   is `'self'` or `http.service_origin`).
2. **Island service** — `rocdown serve-islands DIR` for colocated handlers
   handlers, or `rocci run` on `[http].service`. The CDN file is a snapshot; the
   service owns durable state.

A site with no `live` pages emits only (1). Start the service beside the static
tree; do not serve Markdown from it.

### Cache advice

Hashed `/assets/*` files (theme CSS, images, Datastar.js, `goto.js`) are content-addressed
and can be cached indefinitely. HTML documents, `pages.json`, `islands.json`,
`llms.txt`, `sitemap.xml`, and `robots.txt` should be revalidated: they name the
current hashes and service routes. `pages.json` is the page list used by Cmd/Ctrl-K
fuzzy navigation; it is not full-text site search.

## Build fields

`output` is resolved relative to the content root unless `--output` overrides
it. `assets` names a directory whose files are content-hashed into `/assets/`
in the published tree (`og.png` becomes `og.<hash>.png`). Markdown and `:img`
URLs under that tree are rewritten whether they are authored as
`/assets/foo.png` or `assets/foo.png`. `social_image`, `favicon`, and
`apple_touch_icon` are rewritten the same way.

Local image paths resolve against the source file directory. Missing files fail
`rocdown check` with `RD2103`. Validated page and heading links are rewritten to
emitted routes after catalog resolve, so a passing check implies a usable href.

Rocdown writes into a staging directory and atomically replaces the output only
after a successful build. A failed build leaves the previous output tree in
place. Rebuilding with unchanged inputs produces byte-identical files.

`--host auto|native|wasm` selects how apply runs on the **build machine**.
Native apply uses `basic-cli` without Roc `--target`. Wasm apply is a renderer
only: it does not produce a hosted Wasm server, and platforms such as
`basic-cli` / `basic-webserver` have no `wasm32`, so native apply remains
required. `--target x64musl` / `arm64musl` is the native ISA/OS for island and
app **process** binaries that will run elsewhere (Linux containers). Match the
container CPU (`arm64musl` for Apple Silicon Docker Desktop; `x64musl` for
most amd64 hosts)—not the Mac host (`arm64mac`). Musl is the portable Linux
container target. Do not pass musl `--target` into Mac apply. See
`docker/README.md`.

The first-party `RocdownBase` article CSS is extracted once, hashed, and linked
from every page, including sites with a project-local `theme/`. Project chrome
and named layouts compile beside it. Generated HTML declares the CSP with a
`<meta>` tag. Article HTML must be a component body parameter; a record field
named `content` is escaped as text.

`build.theme` names a directory or `SiteShell.rocci` file relative to the
content root. When omitted, Rocdown uses `theme/` if it contains `.rocci`
files, otherwise the builtin documentation shell.

## Article block packs

`theme/Blocks.rocci`, or `theme/blocks/*.rocci` when that file is absent, can
replace builtin article-block painters (`Note`, `Tabs`, …) by exporting the
same `@component` names. Packs may `import DocsComponents` and wrap a builtin.
`[blocks]` in `rocdown.toml` selects a pack path and optional kind remaps:

```toml
[blocks]
pack = "theme/widgets"
debug = false

[blocks.override]
note = "Callout"
```

- `pack`: optional path, relative to the content root, to a `.rocci` file or a
  directory of `.rocci` files. When set, it replaces the `theme/Blocks.rocci` /
  `theme/blocks/` convention.
- `debug`: when `true`, `rocdown build` and `rocdown check` paint a first-party
  debug placeholder (`data-rocci-block-debug`) for kinds that have a schema but
  no renderer. `rocdown view` always uses that placeholder in preview. Unknown
  kinds stay errors.
- `[blocks.override]`: maps a builtin widget kind (`note`) to a pack or
  `DocsComponents` component name (`Callout`). Unknown kinds fail config load.
  Unknown component names fail theme compile.

A pack `@component` whose name does not match a builtin painter becomes a
custom article kind: PascalCase `Callout` is authored as `:callout`. The
inferred schema uses the `@component` header (`??` defaults and an extra body
parameter). Child policy is fragment plus any children. Helpers and other
non-kind functions must not live in the block pack; they become kinds or
collide with reserved names (`page`, `use`, …).

## Composable content mounts

Sites can mount external documentation catalogs or content trees using `[[mount]]` tables in `rocdown.toml`. This allows maintaining standalone documentation at repository root (`docs/`) while mounting it into a full website (`site/`):

```toml
[[mount]]
source = "../docs"
prefix = "docs"
layout = "docs"

[[mount]]
source = "../dist/example-docs"
prefix = "examples"
layout = "docs"
visibility = "linked-detail"
```

`dist/example-docs` is produced by `rocci-docs` (`cargo run -q -p rocci-docs --
--catalog examples/rocci/apps.toml --output dist/example-docs`). Run that
before `rocdown check site`, `rocdown view site`, or `rocdown package site`.
Rocdown does not import `rocci-docs`. Generated example pages use the docs
layout. `/docs/examples/` is an alias of `/examples/` during the move.

- `source`: Relative path to the mounted content directory.
- `prefix`: Route and page ID prefix (e.g. `prefix = "docs"` maps `five-minutes` to ID `docs/five-minutes` and route `/docs/five-minutes/`).
- `layout`: Optional default layout applied to pages in this mount.
- `visibility`: `navigable` by default. `linked-detail` keeps intentionally
  linked generated details out of navigation without `RD2202` warning noise;
  inspection still reports them as unlisted. Do not use it to hide authored
  pages that should be discoverable.

## Navigation

Each navigation item is a stable page ID derived from the content-root-relative
path without `.rocdown`:

| Source | Page ID | Route |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `index.rocdown` | `index` | `/` |
| `guides/index.rocdown` | `guides/index` | `/guides/` |
| `guides/build.rocdown` | `guides/build` | `/guides/build/` |

Navigation order is explicit. Unknown IDs and duplicate items fail the build.
Published authored pages omitted from navigation emit `RD2202`. Generated
detail mounts may opt into the explicit `linked-detail` policy above.

A directory with `index.rocdown` is a **section**. The sidebar heading may
link to that landing (peel-by-id: the first listed `…/index` becomes the
heading href even when the page title is Overview and the group label is
Start). The first child is a reserved **Overview** row to the same URL. Do
not print `INDEX` or repeat the section title as that row. A nested
`dir/nested/index.rocdown` is a named subsection titled from that page, with
the same Overview-first child. A directory without an index is not a
section; its pages stay flat in the parent. Two or more listed pages in an
indexless directory warn `RD2205` (warning, not an error). Visible nesting
stops at group → subsection → pages. Same-level members share one indent.

Use `[[nav.groups]]` under a top-level `[[nav]]` for curated lanes (Docs →
Start, Templates, …): one header link, left rail folds per group. Do not add
a group merely to recreate a folder; add `index.rocdown` instead. Group items
are the same page IDs. A section with only groups and no `items` is valid.
List each subsection index immediately before its members. Sidebar groups
share the same expandable shape and open independently under user control.
The short transition honors reduced-motion preferences, and explicit
open/closed choices persist while the user navigates in the same browser tab.
The Examples group lists generated application overview pages; generated
source pages remain linked details.

Each page also gets a right-hand “On this page” outline from heading levels 2
and 3. That rail is documentation-site chrome. A single `.rocdown` file opened
with `rocdown view` uses the standalone left navigator instead; see
[Rocdown](/docs/rocdown/language/).

## Layout and site-surface ownership

The catalog validates layout names and supplies page, navigation, breadcrumb,
journey, and outline data. The project theme owns the visible frame. On
rocci.dev, `home` is the only sidebar-free layout. Every other published
page, including FAQ, uses the shared navigated frame. FAQ uses the responsive
page outline for its questions, while Home keeps authored path cards and its
live island in source order.

rocci.dev has no News collection, feed, lane, card, or route in the generated
site. Historical URL redirects and terminal `410` responses are configured at
the production origin, not encoded as catalog prose or theme behavior.

## Repository validation

From the repository root, the complete local content workflow is:

```sh
uv run rocci-ops site
```

It stages `dist/example-docs`, then runs `rocdown check site`, `rocdown test
site`, and `rocdown build site`. Use those focused commands directly while
iterating. Rust changes also require `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and the
narrow owning-crate tests; cross-cutting changes require `cargo test
--workspace`. Knowledge changes require `okmate check
knowledge --profile base`. Build and rebuild failure tests assert that an
existing output tree remains intact when catalog validation, CDN-only policy,
or a watched rebuild fails.

## Generated artifacts

In addition to page HTML, a configured build emits hashed `/assets/` files, a
themed `404.html`, alias redirect HTML, `llms.txt`, and `pages.json`. When
`base_url` is set, it also emits `sitemap.xml` and `robots.txt`. `pages.json`
is the page list used by Cmd/Ctrl-K go-to-page (preview and hosted); it is not
full-text site search. Same-origin in-page links swap already-rendered HTML
unless the target is `live` or otherwise needs a full load. Each entry includes
`kind` (`static`, `hydrate`, or `live`) and `datastar` when Datastar.js was
emitted for that page. Custom `SiteShell` themes should render
`view.resources.chrome_script` so the palette ships.

Sites with `live` pages also emit `islands.json`: `service_origin`, the live
page ids and routes, and the mutation routes the island service serves.

`rocdown inspect artifacts` prints the publish report without invoking Roc:
page kinds, whether Datastar was emitted, service origin and routes, and the
planned output tree including hashed asset paths.

`rocdown package` writes that report as `publish.json` in the output tree, plus
`site.tgz`. Static catalogs imply `--cdn-only`. Hybrid catalogs compile a
sibling `islands` binary and record live routes plus the binary fingerprint
unless `--cdn-only` (then `RD2302`). `rocdown serve DIST` hosts the CDN tree
without rebuilding.

Build this site's content from the repository root with:

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- build docs
```

`docs/` is a `static` catalog. Hybrid fixtures such as
`examples/rocdown/hybrid` and `examples/rocdown/counter` emit CDN HTML plus
`islands.json`; pass `--cdn-only` only when the publish has no island service.
The SQLite counter runbook is [Publish a hybrid Rocdown site](/docs/rocdown/hybrid/).

Preview with watch and live reload:

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- run docs
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- run examples/rocdown/hybrid
```

`rocdown view` writes to a temp directory by default so it does not replace
`build.output`. Pass `--output` to keep the preview files. `--no-window` serves
in the browser only. Static content reloads after a successful rebuild. Hybrid
sites with colocated handlers proxy the island service on the same origin, and
handler edits restart that service. A sibling `[http].service` app is still
started with `rocdown serve-islands` or `rocci run`.

## Open Knowledge Format (OKF)

Knowledge bundles are managed with [okmate](https://github.com/koliyo/okmate) rather than the documentation site generator:

```sh
okmate check knowledge --profile base
okmate view knowledge
```
